# The golden corpus of `design/core.md` §8.5, one JSON message per line.
#
# Each entry is {kind, source, message}. `source` is not decoration: entries
# marked CAPTURED came off the wire from a real server and entries that are not
# were hand-authored from `reference/lsp-3.17/metaModel.json`, and §8.5 wants
# the first kind — "a field that appears in no captured message is untested by
# construction, and that is exactly the long tail". Nothing in
# `tests/differential.rs` knows which a line is, so growing the captured half
# is appending lines.
#
# To capture more of the server half, drive the server over stdio and record
# what it sends back: initialize, then `initialized` and a `didOpen`, then wait
# for the `$/progress` end of its indexing token before asking anything — a
# definition request that arrives first is answered `null`, which looks like a
# real answer and is not one. gopls needs a module with a `go.mod`; pyright
# needs nothing. Ask a position with no definition too, since a real decline is
# a shape of its own.
#
# To capture more of the client half you need an editor, and the editor has to
# be pointed at a script that forwards to the real server and writes every
# frame it forwards to a file. Emacs is the one that drives headless:
# `emacs --batch` with `eglot-server-programs` bound to the proxy,
# `(apply #'eglot--connect (eglot--guess-contact))` rather than `eglot-ensure`
# — which defers to `post-command-hook` and so never fires in batch — and
# `(eglot--signal-textDocument/didChange)` between buffer edits, or eglot
# coalesces them into one notification. The project root needs a `.git` for
# `project-current` to find it without prompting, and a server that does not
# advertise `save` in its `textDocumentSync` will never make the editor send a
# `didSave`.
#
# rust-analyzer, pyright and gopls are all captured, which is the server list
# §8.5 names. The client half is captured too, and it needs a real editor
# rather than a stdio client, because composing an `initialize` and a
# `contentChanges` is what an editor *is*: a recording proxy is put between the
# editor and the server, the editor is driven through open/edit/save/close, and
# the client-to-server frames are the lines. §8.5 names Zed and VS Code; the
# editor here is Emacs, whose eglot is a real LSP client and is the one that
# runs headless. What that leaves untested is per-editor idiosyncrasy, not the
# unions.
